      "text": "To find the area of the shaded region, we first need to calculate the areas of the semicircles. The formula for the area of a semicircle is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi r^2\n\\]\n\nFor arc \\(ADB\\) and arc \\(BEC\\), which each have a radius of 1 unit:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of semicircle } ADB = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of semicircle } BEC = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\nSince both semicircles have the same radius, their areas are equal. Thus, the total area of the two semicircles is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two semicircles} = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi + \\frac{1}{2} \\pi = \\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to consider the semicircle arc \\(DFE\\). The diameter of this arc is the same as the distance between the points \\(D\\) and \\(E\\), which is 2 units because \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) are midpoints of the respective arcs. Therefore, the radius of semicircle \\(DFE\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Radius of semicircle } DFE = \\frac{2}{2} = 1 \\text{ unit}\n\\]\n\nThe area of semicircle \\(DFE\\) is then:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area of semicircle } DFE = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\nnow replace the shaded area with the difference between the intersecting semicircles and the central semicircle \\(DFE\\). Thus, \n\n\\[\n\\text{Total shaded area} = \\pi - \\frac{1}{2} \\pi\n\\]\n\nwe can simplify this expression:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total shaded area} = \\left ( \\frac{1}{2} \\pi + \\frac{1}{2} \\pi - \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\right)+ \\frac{1}{2} \\pi = \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2} \\pi - \\frac{1}{4} \\pi}\n\\]\n\nThe final area of the shaded region is: \n\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}",
