      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the shaded region formed by the intersections of the semicircles. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the radii and the area of each semicircle:**\n   - Each semicircle has a radius of 1 unit.\n   - The area of a full circle with radius 1 unit is \\( \\pi \\times 1^2 = \\pi \\).\n   - Therefore, the area of a semicircle is \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of each semicircle:**\n   - The semicircle \\(ADB\\) has an area of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n   - The semicircle \\(BEC\\) has an area of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n   - The semicircle \\(DFE\\) has an area of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n\n3. **Determine the shaded region:**\n   - The shaded region consists of the areas of the semicircles \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\) minus the area of the semicircle \\(DFE\\).\n   - Since each semicircle \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\) covers 60% of its area, and only partially overlaps with the semicircle \\(DFE\\) covering the rest of its area, the shaded area is effectively the total area of the two full semicircles minus the area of the single full semicircle \\(DFE\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the total shaded area:**\n   - The total area of the two semicircles \\(ADB\\) and \\(BEC\\) is \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\).\n   - The area of the semicircle \\(DFE\\) is \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n   - Therefore, the area of the shaded region is \\( \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n\nThus, the area of the shaded region is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\).",
