      "text": "The total area will be the area of the semicircle plus the area of the four triangles formed by connecting the vertices of each semicircle with the midpoint of the opposite arc.\n\nFirst, we need to figure out the horizontal and vertical lengths of each of the two semicircles.\n\nTo calculate the length of line  $AD$ , we need to examine the properties of the right triangle $ADB$. Because $ADB$ and $BEC$ are semicircles with a radius of 1 unit, that means all sides of triangle $ADB$ will be 1 unit as well.\n\nTo find the length of  $AD$, we apply the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\\begin{align*}\nAD &= \\sqrt{AD^2 + AD^2}\\\\\n& = AD \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n1^2 &= AD \\sqrt{2}\\\\\nAD &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $DC$ is a straight line, it shares the same length as $AD$, which is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\nFinally, to find the height of each of the triangles separately, we need to find the vertical distance between  $B$ and the midpoint of  $AD$. That distance will be equal to the radius of the semicircles minus the remaining vertical distance divided in half. $(\\sqrt{3}/2) - (1/2) = \\sqrt{3} - 1$.\n\nNow, let’s plug these values into the formula for the area of a triangle:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Area of each triangle} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\text{base} \\cdot \\text{height}\\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\cdot (\\sqrt{3} -1)\\\\\n& = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nTo find the area of one of the four triangles, we then multiply this answer by 4 to get $\\frac{2\\sqrt{6} - 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{3}$. We can then find half of this answer to find the total area of the 4 triangles.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Total area of 4 triangles} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nTo find the area of the semicircle, we isolate the radius, square this value, multiply by $\\pi$, and divide by 2 for the semicircle area. \n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Area of each semicircle} &= 4(\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot 1^2)\\\\\n& = \\pi\n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, we take the area of each semicircle minus half the area of the 4 triangles to find the shaded area.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Shaded area} &= (\\pi)\\left(1 - \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n& = \\pi - (\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{6})\\\\\n& = \\boxed{\\pi + \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{6}}\n\\end{align*}\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\pi + \\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{6}}\\)",
